1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,959
The chip in your phone was printed with light made from exploding tin.

2
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:09,018
To draw features this small, chip makers use extreme ultraviolet light,

3
00:00:09,018 --> 00:00:12,685
with a wavelength of just thirteen point five nanometers.

4
00:00:13,183 --> 00:00:13,867
To make it,

5
00:00:13,867 --> 00:00:18,402
a machine fires about fifty thousand droplets of molten tin every second,

6
00:00:18,402 --> 00:00:20,327
and hits each one with a laser.

7
00:00:20,817 --> 00:00:24,123
The tin turns into plasma hotter than the surface of the Sun,

8
00:00:24,123 --> 00:00:26,129
and that plasma glows with the light.

9
00:00:26,617 --> 00:00:29,621
Air absorbs that light, so it all runs in a vacuum,

10
00:00:29,621 --> 00:00:33,215
off mirrors so smooth that, scaled up to the size of Germany,

11
00:00:33,215 --> 00:00:36,161
the biggest bump would be a tenth of a millimeter.

12
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:39,673
Just one company, ASML, builds these machines.

13
00:00:39,673 --> 00:00:44,340
The newest model costs around three hundred and eighty million dollars.

14
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:46,832
So what's inside the chips it makes?

15
00:00:46,832 --> 00:00:48,263
Full story on the channel.
